WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.520
Alight with bioluminescence as&nbsp;
they float through the night sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.520
the firefly is a mystical, enchanting&nbsp;
creature for many cultures.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.320
But not so for the West African Ewe&nbsp;
peoples of Ghana, Benin, Nigeria, and Togo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.680
For them, fireflies can cause great anxiety.
In Ewe mythology, fireflies may be the form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.320
taken by an evil entity called the Adze.&nbsp;
While there are very few folk stories about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.880
the ‘Adze’ as a physical monster, mostly&nbsp;
just passing references and descriptions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.280
‘adze’ as a belief is more complex.
So why has the idea of a flying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:39.120
nocturnal, blood-sucking firefly endured?
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka. And this is Monstrum.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.760
A nebulous monstrous spirit, adze can take&nbsp;
on many guises, the firefly among them. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.200
Ewe peoples believe that this nocturnal force is&nbsp;
almost always ill-intentioned, and if called upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.760
through malevolent sorcery, may enter the body&nbsp;
and empower the sorcerer with special abilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.920
While under the influence of malignant adze, these&nbsp;
individuals are called an adzetos or azetowos.&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.880
While possessed, the adzetos are compelled&nbsp;
into nocturnal shapeshifting. A floating ball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.840
of light or a flame emerges from the possessed&nbsp;
person’s nose or mouth or may appear through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.280
top of their head. Often, the flame takes the&nbsp;
form of an owl, firefly, or a kind of beetle.&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.760
This allows the possessed to travel great&nbsp;
distances by flight, which comes in handy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.880
because the spirit also compels the adzetos&nbsp;
to feed on the life force of other humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.600
usually their blood or their flesh.
The word ‘adze’ however itself has a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.640
of a complex etymology, especially amongst&nbsp;
west African people like Theon the mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:45.600
the Shante and so many others who live&nbsp;
in Togo, Benin, Ghana, and Nigeria.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.680
That’s Helen Nde, researcher and&nbsp;
curator of Mythological Africans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.000
an online community that explores mythology&nbsp;
and folklore from the African continent.&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.200
In one context, as they are understood&nbsp;
as a creative genius and spiritual power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:09.520
especially as embodied and wielded by women,&nbsp;
Adze&nbsp;is also understood as a bad sorcery,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.400
so creative&nbsp;genius and spiritual&nbsp;
power wielded for harm. So&nbsp;to hurt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.200
other people out of jealousy or envy, or&nbsp;
just sudden for design to see them suffer.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.120
Helen taught me that all adze aren’t&nbsp;
bad or&nbsp;&nbsp;malevolent. It's more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.920
the intention of the person who wields this force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.840
The capital “A” Adze monster we are talking about&nbsp;
is just one manifestation, a very frightening one.&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.840
The monster Adze’s ability to appear as an&nbsp;
insect makes it particularly threatening.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.880
They could be anywhere and everywhere.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.960
Their small size allows them to enter&nbsp;
seemingly closed spaces through keyholes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.360
cracks, and under door frames. And&nbsp;
insect-borne illness is already a threat.&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.120
In some variations of Adze lore, the&nbsp;
monsters have an appendage like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.160
elephant trunk or ant-eater -like nose used&nbsp;
to slurp up the blood of their victims.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.800
If blood isn’t handy, the creatures will drink&nbsp;
palm oil, coconut water, or coconut milk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.280
so these fluids can be offered to appease&nbsp;
an Adze monster and protect one’s community.&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.040
Flying, nocturnal, blood-sucking&nbsp;
insects—you would think the mosquito&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.800
would be the most common form, so why fireflies?
If you start with the understanding of as a, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.400
a flame-like entity, which comes out of people's&nbsp;
noses and mouths or out the top of their heads and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.760
flies around at night, looking for other people&nbsp;
to stop blood from and cause death or disease&nbsp;to

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.880
then it makes sense that the firefly would be one&nbsp;
of the creatures associated with this entity. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.880
flies around at night, it glows in the dark.
A flying insect that appears to glow like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.320
a small flame makes sense. It’s also possible&nbsp;
that the belief in the monstrous Adze developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.040
as a response not only to illness more&nbsp;
broadly but to disease-carrying insects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.280
like mosquitoes, which could explain the&nbsp;
proboscis-like appendage of the creature.&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.720
But let’s not forget, ‘adze’ mostly refers&nbsp;
to malevolent sorcery, not a monster.&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.680
The main misconception about that, I would&nbsp;
like to dispel is this understanding of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:08.400
as simply a blood sucking vampiric entity. Adze&nbsp;
cannot be understood outside of its context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.960
as a manifestation of bad sorcery. So you have the&nbsp;
entity, but the entity comes out of the efforts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:22.160
a bad actor using occult powers. Um, blood sucking&nbsp;
is also just one of the ways in which they will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:28.240
break havoc on a person's life. They also cause&nbsp;
illness and death. They cause mental distress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:33.680
and they can cause loss of job, loss of property,&nbsp;
or just lots of good standing in society.&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.240
Ewe practitioners of this bad sorcery, in&nbsp;
general,&nbsp;may be associated with cannibalism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.600
consumption because they prey on the resources&nbsp;
of the community to the detriment of all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.160
Often associated with contentious familial&nbsp;
and community relationships, in Ewe culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.880
adze attacks pose a constant threat, most&nbsp;
predominantly to wealth and&nbsp;&nbsp;fertility.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.640
And another valuable resource—blood.
The Adze monster’s preferred victims are children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.880
In fact, in some Ewe traditions, they&nbsp;
only hunt the most beautiful children.&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.640
My theory around this is that physical&nbsp;
attractiveness is, uh, an enviable quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:14.720
And one of the motivating forces behind using&nbsp;
Adze is jealousy and envy and wanting other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.160
people to suffer. So, uh, people who&nbsp;
have qualities that another person envy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.400
become targets for Adze, and it's not just beauty.&nbsp;
There is intelligence and charisma and wealth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.840
and anything that a person might possess that&nbsp;
other people might, might, might want or feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:36.400
jealous towards them and want them to suffer for.
The Adze are active at night, and some people say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.040
they see them in their dreams. If you manage to&nbsp;
catch one of these creatures, it will transform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.600
into a human and attack you. So it’s possible&nbsp;that&nbsp;
they can be killed in this mortal form.&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.560
They believe, however, that if you were to catch&nbsp;
an Adze&nbsp;and a transforming into a human being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.600
it will plead for its life and offer you all kinds&nbsp;
of things to make sure that you don't kill&nbsp;it.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.480
And if you spirits life, then it becomes your&nbsp;
friend and it doesn't bother you anymore.&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.200
There are groups within Ewe culture that&nbsp;
work against the use of malevolent sorcery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.560
neutralizing or punishing the&nbsp;
accused—sometimes with death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.320
Someone can be accused of being a blood-sucking,&nbsp;
possessed firefly or a traveling flame and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.960
look like a human during the day—and therefore&nbsp;
there’s a possibility that real innocent lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.560
can be taken in the name of Adze destruction.
Long a part of oral traditions, one of the Adze’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.320
first appearances in written literature&nbsp;
is in an ethnographic book written in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:34.320
1906 by German missionary Jakob Spieth.
While his description isn’t incorrect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.360
he is very quick to label adze as&nbsp;
‘witchcraft,” a complicated term in Africa.&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.600
Over time, you know, with colonization&nbsp;
and the introduction of Christianity and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.520
and other spiritual belief systems. The&nbsp;
fear of Adze has only become more complex.&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.000
Now it is impossible to situate this outside&nbsp;
of the context of colonialism, because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:01.360
what happened with the wave of colonial enterprise&nbsp;
that swept across much of the African continent is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.800
that traditional ways of organizing community&nbsp;
belief systems and political systems were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.920
destabilized. And this had a profound effect&nbsp;
on the physical, emotional, and psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:18.320
wellbeing of communities. And there is research&nbsp;
which shows that, where this, this destabilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:24.000
brought by colonialism, the inequities&nbsp;
and operations caused by that, that system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.520
where they, where they happened, there is an&nbsp;
increase in complex beliefs, such as witchcraft.&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.840
Helen and I both found that identifying the&nbsp;
Adze as a vampiric spirit doesn’t occur until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.200
the 20th century, in Matthew Bunson’s&nbsp;
1993 book The Vampire Encyclopedia.&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:44.320
So the Adze gained the “vampire” moniker not&nbsp;
from the culture from which it originated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:49.120
but from an attempt to contextualize the&nbsp;entity&nbsp;
to a broader, largely Western,&nbsp;audience.&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.400
It’s so important that we trace back to&nbsp;
and understand the origins of this monster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.360
Without that the true meaning is lost and muddled.&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.000
There is a resurgence in mythology and&nbsp;
folklore from the African continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.360
which is good, but it also carries the risk&nbsp;
of misunderstanding and misinterpretation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.960
especially since many of the documented sources of&nbsp;
these mythologies and folklores that are available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.640
were done by people who are not themselves&nbsp;
African. So, it is well known that myths and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:25.040
folklore are not just stories. They are containers&nbsp;
of cultural beliefs and values and practices. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:31.040
and so they are used to preserve and plum&nbsp;
to expand cultural knowledge and practices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:36.720
And I believe that myths and folk will offer a&nbsp;
great opportunity to learn what we have thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.680
about ourselves in the past and explore the&nbsp;
possibilities of who we can be in the future.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:46.400
I couldn’t have said it better myself.&nbsp;&nbsp;Studying&nbsp;
monsters isn’t about what you think you&nbsp;know

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:50.720
because of something you’ve seen or read,&nbsp;&nbsp;but&nbsp;
investigating what that creature means 

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.010
to the&nbsp;&nbsp;culture in which it was created.

